================
DHCP Starvation Mitigation

Why we did this
================
A DHCP Starvation Attack floods the DHCP Server with thousands of DHCP requests using fake MAC addresses.

As a result:
- DHCP pool becomes exhausted.
- Legitimate users cannot obtain IP addresses.
- Network connectivity is lost.
- Attackers may introduce a Rogue DHCP Server.

To protect the network from DHCP pool exhaustion, Cisco introduced DHCP Starvation Mitigation using DHCP Snooping Rate Limiting.
This limits the number of DHCP requests a device can send.

What will happen
================
1. User ports are configured as Untrusted.
2. DHCP rate limits are applied.
3. Switch monitors DHCP packets.
4. If DHCP requests exceed the configured threshold:
   - Excess packets are dropped.
   - Security violation occurs.
   - Port may become Err-Disabled.
5. DHCP Server remains protected.
6. Legitimate users continue to receive IP addresses.

